  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">RCRC partnership with the UN</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the General Assembly in 2024, NLRC co-hosted a UN Partnerships side event, reinforcing its commitment to equitable collaboration. This event marked the launch of the Blue-Red Pillar Study, developed in partnership with the IFRC and seven NSs. The study focuses on strengthening UN partnerships and improving data collection to better understand funding flows and advocate for systematic change.
The primary objective of this study is to conduct a detailed mapping exercise to assess the current state of partnerships between Red Cross Red Crescent National Societies and UN agencies. This will include:
&#x2022;	Quantitative data collection on funding flows between UN agencies and National Societies, including bilateral funding and various pooled funds.
&#x2022;	Qualitative analysis of the quality of the funding (terms, conditions), and nature of these partnerships.
&#x2022;	Identification of challenges, opportunities, and best practices in these partnerships</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Study on collaboration between RCRC and the UN: This study focuses on strengthening UN partnerships and enhancing data collection to provide clearer insights into funding flows and identify areas where stronger advocacy is needed to drive systemic change in the humanitarian sector. The RCRC Movement, however, recognises that it cannot achieve this transformation alone. Real progress depends on the active engagement of partners who need to accelerate their efforts and translate commitments into tangible action</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The study consists of 3 phases: Phase 1: the first 6 months will be solely for data extraction and monitoring. Phase 2: analysis and peer to peer exchange: By using the Community of Practice, the SOL will work on discovering other challenges that the NSs are facing in working with the UN (for example, visibility, lack of trainings etc.). Phase 3: creating key messages, narratives and empowering HD on the topic.</narrative>
  </description>
